CONTENT = {
'00': '''
Tiers of evidence｜The work itself, primary documents, later scholarship and personal inference answer different questions. Collection labels also get revised: tie every claim back to a specific record rather than remembering only a website\'s name.｜“Red symbolizes power” cannot be derived from red alone; check the work\'s use, local symbol conventions and the relevant literature.｜Build an evidence table of five claims for one work, marking each as work evidence, primary document, scholarly interpretation or to-be-verified.｜At least one claim is actively narrowed because its evidence is insufficient.
Multiple timelines of art history｜Building parallel timelines by region, medium and institution avoids mistaking one style\'s changes for the whole world\'s history. When comparing, look at simultaneity as well as transmission and non-synchronization.｜Two works from the same century may serve ritual and the market respectively; “more realistic” does not directly imply “more advanced.”｜Choose three collection objects from each of two regions, arrange two timelines by date, and record materials and uses.｜Similarities that cannot be verified are not written up as transmission or influence.
Making theory face counterexamples｜Formal analysis, social context and viewing experience are different ways of asking. Likewise, the theory of imitation asks what a work represents, expression theory asks what feeling it expresses, formalism asks whether form itself carries meaning, and institutional theory asks how the art world confers status; theory helps select evidence and also leaves material out. Using it well is not applying a label but stating what it explains and what it cannot.｜For a poster, formal analysis can discuss hierarchy and institutional analysis can discuss commissioning and circulation; neither automatically substitutes for audience research.｜Write two 120-word analyses of the same work from different methods, then list each method\'s blind spots.｜Each passage carries at least two pieces of evidence, not just the theory\'s name.
From topic to research question｜“Studying Chinese painting” is too broad; an operable question delimits object, period, materials and the comparison at stake. Pilot a small sample first, then decide whether the question deserves scaling up.｜Turn “how does the public see art” into “do two label texts in this exhibition change the kinds of questions three readers ask.” A small sample reflects only this test.｜Write out the question, the scope of materials, three source entry points and the evidence that could overturn the hypothesis; pilot the analysis on two objects.｜The question can be answered by available materials and allows a negative result.
Curating is an argument｜An exhibition\'s selection, sequence, labels and omissions all state a position. First write one discussible claim, then state for each chosen work what evidential role it carries.｜Five works about “waiting” should not merely share a title; they can embody body, space, time and institution respectively.｜Make a six-work exhibition on paper with a 150-word thesis, 60-word labels for each work, a route map and a loan-substitute plan.｜Every inclusion rationale ties back to the claim, and image-use status is noted.
The researcher\'s independent judgment｜Advanced research builds traceable arguments, answers counter-evidence and states its own position. “Originality” can be new material, a new comparison or a revision of an old interpretation — there is no need to claim you overturn all of art history.｜A gap in a work\'s provenance record may change the interpretation; the gap itself is not evidence of forgery.｜Complete a 2,500-word thematic study or a research exhibition of ten works, with notes, a materials catalog, opposing views and one revision statement.｜Invite someone with relevant experience to review; answer the strongest objection and mark what remains unresolved.
''',
'01': '''
Value before detail｜Compressing the picture into light, middle and dark groups checks whether subject and background separate. Detail can hide structural problems, so squint or shrink the image first, then decide where edges must be crisp.｜A complex street scene shrunk to a thumbnail may crush signage and figures into one dark mass; finely painted parts still fail to create hierarchy.｜Paint six three-value thumbnails with a fixed subject while changing the areas of light and dark; pick one and explain where the eye lands first.｜The six are not detail variations but genuinely different value structures.
Color is a relationship｜How a color reads depends on its neighbors, its value and its area; the same color may look different against another ground. Limiting the palette and comparing adjacent relations beats chasing a “standard sky blue.”｜A gray patch on a bright ground and on a dark ground may look different; screen and paint do not map one-to-one either.｜Paint two three-color versions of the same still life, swapping background values; record the actual mixtures and the differences observed.｜Point to at least two neighboring-color relations; avoid equating warm and cool with fixed emotions.
Edges and the rhythm of looking｜Crisp, soft and lost edges steer attention; they work together with value and material. Edge treatment should serve the viewing relationship, not render the whole painting equally sharp.｜Equally sharp contours everywhere may flatten near-far relations; easing contrast away from the focus can reorganize the reading.｜Mark three kinds of edges in an original work, then make two pencil studies with different edge handling.｜You can say how the edge changes alter looking; blur is not treated as a universal mark of sophistication.
From copy to variation｜Analytical copying extracts one problem only — composition or brushwork, say; the variation keeps that problem and changes the subject to test it. Both must credit their source; a copy is not independent original work.｜Keeping a work\'s big value pattern while switching the motif to your own desktop tests whether you truly understood the organizing method.｜Choose a work with clear collection documentation; complete a structural copy, a subject-changed variation and a 300-word difference analysis.｜The variation is not a color swap; explain where the original method fails in the new subject.
Consistency and change in a series｜A series is held together by a shared question, not by repeating one look. Fix the constants first, then let each piece carry a different experiment; the question may be revised midway, but keep the process.｜“One window at four distances” is easier to compare for space and viewing than “four arbitrary paintings.”｜Complete four to six works, keeping studies, failures and material notes, and write each piece\'s contribution to the overall question.｜Eliminate at least one redundant or ineffective piece and say why.
Masterwork study and a personal language｜Studying mature works means analyzing choices and their costs: what a work emphasizes and what it deliberately gives up. A personal language accumulates through long choosing; it cannot be speeded up by copying a signature brushstroke.｜Studying one painter across three periods shows how the method changed; a single image cannot be generalized into that painter\'s whole style.｜From reliable collections choose three works by one artist plus one contrast from a different tradition; write a 1,200-word study and finish a six-piece personal series.｜The final series states its borrowings, departures and revisions; a second version follows feedback from someone with practical experience.
''',
'02': '''
Making strokes comparable｜Fix the character, the size and the tool first, and compare one variable only. The brush\'s spring, the ink\'s density and the paper\'s character (raw xuan absorbs and bleeds quickly; sized xuan does not) together shape what a stroke can be; only with tools and materials fixed can variables of hand be compared. Launch, travel and finish form the observation frame; appearance alone cannot certify how much force a brush method “must” use.｜The thick-to-thin transition of one horizontal stroke can be observed, but the exact motion needs demonstration and practice to verify.｜Write one simple character 20 times with an ordinary pen or brush; group and compare width, direction and turns — do not pile up sheets for quantity.｜Name two differences you can reproduce stably, not just the single best-looking sheet.
Density inside a single character｜Character structure is not filling the square: the spacing of parts, the balance point and the extensions form relationships. Choose a clear model first, mark the spaces before copying, then check the deviation afterward.｜A left-right compound can balance unequal stroke counts through placement and scale.｜Choose five characters from one stele or modelbook; for each make a spacing diagram, a copy and a correction note.｜Corrections land on concrete part relations, not on “lacks spirit.”
Steles, modelbooks and version awareness｜Original ink traces, carved editions, rubbings, tracing copies and modern printed images pass through different mediations. The edges and ink you see may not all come from the author\'s hand; check the version before studying.｜After a stone inscription is worn, rubbed and printed, the character edges may change; a low-resolution thumbnail cannot settle claims about original brushpoint detail.｜Choose one stele or modelbook with institutional notes and build a version card; compare two images and mark which differences cannot yet be attributed.｜The image carrier, its source and the unverifiable parts are stated.
Rhythm in overall composition｜Good single characters do not make a valid whole; column spacing, character spacing, size, ink and the blanks set an overall tempo. Design the spatial skeleton first, then check whether local strokes support it.｜Four uniformly even columns and alternating dense-sparse columns produce different tempos; variation is not automatically superior to evenness.｜Write one legally usable short text in three overall compositions, keeping the same sheet size, with thumbnail comparisons attached.｜You can explain each version\'s column flow and blank relations, not just single characters.
The seal face and writing, back and forth｜In seal carving, seal-script lettering, layout and the knife-stone relation interlock. Beginners can use paper and seal paste to understand positive-negative form; real carving waits for tools and guidance.｜With the same characters, a different border and different red-white areas shift the seal\'s balance; irregular is not the same as careless.｜Make four paper seal drafts paired with one short piece of calligraphy; compare placement, scale and the pictorial relation.｜The lettering basis can be explained; drafts are not computer lettering stretched at will.
Entering creation through the classics｜Study one script style over the long term while using a second as contrast, and your own habits become visible. A finished work needs text, writing and overall form to hold together; imitating a master\'s surface is not the same as forming judgment.｜The same short poem sets different rhythms in different scripts; poetic mood alone cannot decide that one script is the only fit.｜Study three institutionally documented works by one calligrapher; complete one copy set, one assembled-character set and one independent writing set, with composition and version notes.｜Someone familiar with the script names concrete problems; before-and-after versions are kept after revision — no self-declared mastery.
''',
'03': '''
Balance and negative space｜Sculpture is not only solid; gaps and piercings shape the contour and the sense of support. Paper models test visual relations but cannot be used to infer the safety of real large structures.｜Two identical paper shapes set close together form a narrow channel; pulled apart, the gap becomes open space.｜Make three paper models on a fixed material budget, varying solid versus void; photograph each from four directions.｜Observation from four sides supports your conclusion, not only the most flattering photo.
Surfaces record process｜Cutting, modeling, joining and casting leave different traces. Describe the trace first, then research the process; similar surfaces can come from different processes.｜Wood grain may follow the form\'s direction; a metal surface may deliberately imitate soft material — photos alone cannot judge touch.｜Choose two collection sculptures; record material, process notes and three surface traces, then do one paper or clay experiment.｜Real process documentation and visual inference are recorded in separate columns.
Scale and viewer position｜Scale is decided jointly by object, body and environment. Plinths, barriers and entrances limit viewing distance; when analyzing photos, record which bodily experience is missing.｜A tabletop model enlarged beyond a single glance may break the original compositional relations.｜Make three display plans for a paper model, marking viewer height, distance and route; state one plan\'s limitation.｜Scale references are used; visual pressure is not read as every viewer\'s feeling.
Site survey before installation｜For site works, observe entrances, light, sound, rules of use and existing activity. Understand how the space is being used before deciding whether the work should occupy it.｜A quiet reading area and a corridor put different demands on sound and lingering; the same installation should not simply be transplanted.｜Survey an observable space; make time-slot records and a paper installation plan without actually occupying public ground.｜The plan says how it responds to existing users and to the audience\'s exit path.
Making an installation testable｜A prototype should test a definite question, such as whether viewers notice a relation. Recording real behavior against the author\'s expectation beats collecting “very striking” comments.｜If the author expects viewers to walk around and they simply step across, the spatial cue may not work.｜Build a tabletop or indoor prototype; have three people experience it, recording routes, pauses and questions, then revise once.｜Observation records are kept apart from interpretation; three people are not generalized to all viewers.
An independent spatial project｜A mature project connects material, site, viewing and maintenance. The work must also be installable, documentable, removable and re-understandable; an artist\'s statement cannot replace physical experience.｜For a work relying on a specific echo, photographic records miss the key experience; the archive needs a floor plan and sound notes.｜Study three institutionally documented installations or sculptures; complete a prototype, an installation manual, an experience record and a removal plan.｜A practitioner reviews form and feasibility; revise per feedback and keep the process archive.
''',
'04': '''
Reading plans from body size｜Spatial reading starts with actions: entering, turning, sitting, looking. Plans need a scale reference, and blank paper does not guarantee usable space.｜A door swing and a pulled-out chair both occupy space; ignoring these actions makes a room spacious only on paper.｜Draw a familiar room to rough scale, marking three action clearances; separate estimates from measurements.｜Dimensions and action positions correspond; the exercise drawing is not a construction document.
Sections explain light and level changes｜Plans show horizontal relations; sections expose height, openings and layering. Daylight is temporal — a single rendering cannot show the whole day.｜High windows and side windows may form different light distributions; the actual result also depends on orientation, weather and surrounding obstructions.｜Draw a plan and one section of the same room; record light three times — morning, noon, evening — noting the conditions.｜Drawings and field notes cross-check each other; the result is not just an abstract mood.
Public and private along the circulation｜Entrances, turns and boundaries organize approach and retreat. Usability studies must consider different bodies and use tasks; not everyone moves like the designer.｜A transparent entrance is easy to find and may increase the sense of being watched; the design must name the occasion and the people it serves.｜Make two circulation schemes for a small reading room; compare wayfinding, lingering, quiet and exit.｜At least one trade-off is named that real users would need to verify further.
Landscape has seasons and maintenance｜Plants, water, ground surfaces and paths together form a landscape, and the result changes with season and upkeep. Judge not from completion photos alone but from years of use and resource needs.｜A seat shaded in summer may be cold and dark in winter; the same scheme must be checked at different times.｜Survey a small park; record shade, seating, surfaces and signs of upkeep, and draw a seasonal-problem map.｜Improvements answer observable problems; no pretended engineering calculations.
From need to scheme iteration｜Define the use task and the constraints first, then propose several schemes. Write the comparison criteria before choosing, so you do not fall in love with a look and reason backward.｜If the goal is brief stays, paths, seats and shade should enter the comparison earlier than decorative form.｜Design a paper community rest point with three plan-and-section sketches, a criteria table and one physical paper model.｜The chosen scheme openly states what it sacrificed; items still needing professional verification are marked.
A critical study of masterworks of architecture｜Study the drawings, the use, the construction and the later reception together. Famous works had specific conditions; moving a form to a different climate or society is not “borrowing.”｜An open space in photographs may carry management and access restrictions in reality; check documents rather than infer from images.｜Choose a building with drawings and institutional records; write a spatial study proposing two competing interpretations, then a small adaptation scheme.｜The adaptation states climate, user and site differences; professionals review it; the result belongs to a study scheme.
''',
'05': '''
Write the design brief first｜A design brief states who, in what situation, does what, and by which criteria. Style requirements may exist but cannot replace task and constraints.｜“Make an upscale cup” is hard to test; “easy to recognize and hold for gloved hands” generates concrete tests.｜Write a brief for an everyday object; interview one real user, separating their words from your conjectures.｜At least one requirement comes from observation, not from the designer imagining the user.
Material samples and process records｜Samples isolate process variables: thickness, joining, surface finishing — change one at a time. Keeping failed samples with their conditions is what builds repeatable experience.｜Paper grain and fold direction affect the result; old dimensions cannot be reused directly after switching materials.｜Make six joining and surface samples in paper or cloth; number them with material and steps; choose two to compare.｜Records are good enough that you could redo it next week, not just keep the pretty results.
Task-testing visual hierarchy｜Layout conveys relations through contrast, spacing and grouping. Test with concrete lookup tasks, recording time and misreadings, not just likes.｜A poster can be tested on “when, where, how to join”; decorative preference and task success must be read separately.｜Make two poster versions; have three people find three key pieces of information; record the errors and adjust one version.｜The revision answers actual misreadings; small-sample conclusions stay within this test.
The life of a product｜Craft and design also involve material sourcing, manufacture, use, repair and disposal. Where quantification fails, draw a system map and mark the unknowns; do not casually claim environmental virtue.｜A reusable item also has making and cleaning costs; a material\'s name alone cannot settle the total impact.｜Draw a life-cycle map for one object; list three maintenance modes and two data gaps; propose one repairable change.｜Environmental superiority without data is not written as a conclusion.
A prototype is not a shrunken product｜Prototypes answer questions: paper mockups test layout, feel models test grip, wireframes test flow. Different test purposes need different fidelity.｜A polished shell may not test the internal operation at all; a rough flow model may expose problems faster.｜Run three prototype rounds around one task, each round posing a single question; record the tests and the reasons for changes.｜Each round yields new evidence; beautification does not substitute for functional improvement.
Mastercase studies and independent design｜Studying fine craft or design means looking at production conditions, constraints and use feedback. Your own scheme may inherit principles but should set its own task and criteria.｜A classic chair\'s lightness may rest on specific craft; copying only the outline drops the key logic.｜Choose three related objects from the V&A for case cards; complete a brief, samples, three prototype rounds, tests and display notes.｜Users and experienced makers give separate feedback; concept, functional and production versions are clearly distinguished.
''',
'06': '''
Keeping an exposure log｜Exposure is decided jointly by aperture, shutter speed and sensitivity: aperture governs the opening and depth of field, shutter the time and motion blur, sensitivity the gain and noise; changing one can be offset with the other two, but each one\'s side effects stay in the picture. Controlled comparisons on a fixed scene — watching highlights, shadows and motion traces — beat memorizing a universal parameter set.｜Photographing a moving finger: a slow shutter may smear it; opening the aperture blurs the background; raising sensitivity adds noise — these side effects are exactly the clues for identifying parameters. In low light the device may also change other parameters automatically.｜Shoot six frames of the same scene, recording which parameters your device lets you control; write out the automatic ones explicitly.｜You can say which variables were controlled and which results cannot be attributed to a single cause.
Light direction and texture｜Observe where the source sits relative to the object, then the shadows, reflections and background separation. Soft-hard and direction are different dimensions; “natural light” does not summarize all effects.｜The same cup turned at a window: surface texture and reflections may change even when brightness barely does.｜Shoot the same object in front, side and backlight, keeping one wide frame per set to show the conditions.｜Judgments cite visible shadows and reflections, not mood words.
Editing a set of photographs｜A photo essay earns meaning from selection, sequence and spacing. One strong frame may not work inside a series; editing judges redundant information and narrative jumps.｜Six similar compositions may sustain one rhythm or be mere redundancy — judge against the theme.｜From 20 of your own photos choose six, order them two ways, and ask one person to describe the story each order makes.｜You explain the rejections and note where viewers\' understanding differed from your intent.
Captions and the documentary boundary｜Documentary work distinguishes the on-site record, staging, reenactment and later modification. Captions state the conditions that affect understanding; no moving story fills a factual gap.｜No third person in the frame does not prove only two people were present; the crop boundary must be recognized.｜Write factual captions for the six-photo set, plus a modification note and a consent record or display restriction.｜Staging and unverified inference are never dressed up as on-the-spot fact.
Photobooks and exhibition viewing｜Screen swipes, page turns and wall viewing set different tempos. Output size, sequence and caption placement change the experience; test with low-cost samples.｜A double-page spread emphasizes one picture; a run of small ones builds a sequence; meaning still depends on content relations.｜Make a 12-page paper dummy or a small wall model; test reading order and text legibility.｜Layout choices are justified by their effect; template uniformity does not replace editorial judgment.
A long-term photography project｜A mature project needs steady access to a subject, repeated shooting and self-correction. When studying established photographers, analyze the pictures but also the right of choice, the shooting relation and the editing process.｜One visit\'s impressions should not stand for a whole community; long projects also keep the parts where subjects said no.｜Study three reliably sourced photo works, then complete a personal series with at least three shoots, contact sheets, an edit and a reflection.｜Subjects or experienced photographers review the relevant parts; revisions and unresolved representativeness are stated.
''',

'07': '''
Grounding Close Reading in Words and Sentences｜Close reading requires pointing precisely to words, sentences, repetitions, or the position of a passage. After quoting, explain how the quoted words support the claim; an excerpt cannot stand in for analysis.｜A verb that recurs may build an obsession or a rhythm; you must still check for counterexamples and shifts in context.｜Choose a short poem, make five annotations, propose two interpretations, and find both supporting and opposing evidence for each.｜Quotations are locatable and interpretations return to the words, rather than drifting into general talk about life.
Desire and Obstacle in the Scene｜Narrative scenes often produce change through a character's goal, action, and obstruction. Characterization can be distinguished as flat or round: a flat character revolves around a single trait, while a round character changes and surprises in a convincing way; dialogue is also characterization—who interrupts and who deflects establishes character more surely than a string of adjectives. Not every story needs strong conflict, but track what the reader knows before and after each paragraph.｜A person waiting for a letter can be carried by nothing more than repeated glances at the door; whether it works depends on variation in the details.｜Write a 500-word scene without directly naming the character's emotions; mark the goal, the obstacle, and one change.｜The change happens through action and detail, not through an ending that explains all the psychology.
Point of View and Narrative Reliability｜How much the narrator can know and is willing to say may sit at a distance from what the text implies. Unreliable narration should be analyzed from contradictions or gaps in information; do not judge narration unreliable simply because it is in the first person.｜A character who claims never to be late, while later passages show him repeatedly rescheduling, may create a tension that can actually be analyzed.｜Write one event twice, 400 words from each of two points of view, and list the facts each version cannot know.｜The two versions differ through access to information, not merely through swapped pronouns.
Structure and Revision｜Revision can address paragraph function first and sentences second. A basic distinction of narrative time: events arranged in the order they occurred form the story, and events organized in the order of telling form the plot; flashback, interpolated narration, and switches between scene and summary are all knobs you can adjust directly while revising. Every paragraph should carry a function in narrative, imagery, or argument; deleting a beautiful but functionless sentence is also a choice.｜A passage of background that blocks a key action can be moved later or compressed rather than deleted outright.｜Cut an older piece into paragraph cards, label each function, and try two orderings; submit the original, the revision, and a 300-word rationale.｜Every move is justified by its effect on the reading, not just by a claim that it flows better.
Reading Across Versions and Cultures｜A translation is one concrete version; when comparing, record the translator and publication information. Similar themes in different texts do not prove identical cultural experience; analyze each language and context first.｜Two poems on the moon may organize time, distance, or ritual differently; do not assign them one emotion merely because they share an image.｜Choose two short works, pose one shared question, and write a 1,200-word comparison, noting the editions and quoting locatable passages.｜The comparison shows connections and also marks what cannot be equated.
Masterworks and Personal Writing｜Studying a mature writer means tracking specific choices, not searching for writing secrets. Independent writing needs sustained drafting, outside readers, and structural revision; style is not a handful of catchphrases.｜Copying the surface length of long sentences may miss their internal layering and release of information; analyze how the sentence works.｜Read three short works by one author closely, then complete a set of imitation experiments plus an independent work of 3,000 words or more or a sequence of poems, with two rounds of revision.｜Have a reader recount the changes they actually registered and revise on that basis; note what you borrowed and keep your own experience and choices.
''',
'08': '''
Writing Rhythm as a Reproducible Record｜Marking sounds and rests on a beat grid can turn what you hear into a reproducible pattern. Notation is first a record of relationships, and terminology should serve listening and doing.｜Sounding only on beats one and three of a four-beat measure and sounding on all four beats share the same reference pulse, yet form different rhythmic densities.｜Write four rhythms in a four-beat grid, reproduce them by clapping, and check the recording for whether the steady pulse held.｜The pulse holds through the rests, and changes in speed are not mistaken for changes in the rhythmic pattern.
Direction and Rest in Melody｜A melody can be observed through contour, repetition, the span of its intervals, and its phrase endings. A scale divides the octave into a number of steps; pentatonic and seven-note major and minor are different divisions, and a melody's color and tension come first from which scale system it moves within. While you cannot yet read staff notation, mark the line with a simple graph; as you go deeper, check the pitch relationships step by step.｜Repeating a short phrase and then moving upward may create a sense of extension, but its exact function depends on mode, harmony, and context.｜Choose a short melody you can listen to repeatedly, draw its contour, mark repetitions and rests, then hum a variant.｜The analysis maps onto specific points in time, rather than just attaching happy or sad labels.
Layered Listening and Texture｜First separate the parts and functions sounding at once: main melody, bass, rhythm, or sustained background. Harmony is the vertical sounding-together: three to five notes stack into a chord, and the successive progression of chords builds tension and resolution around a tonal center, while the color contrast between major and minor is the first step in hearing tonality. Texture is not a fixed instrument list; the same instrument can take different roles.｜Adding a sustained tone beneath a melody changes its sense of space and stability; what changes is not only the number of sounds.｜Choose one minute of audio; listen three times, noting melody, bass, and rhythm in turn, and draw a layered timeline; on a fourth pass, try to mark where the chords change or where the light-dark (major-minor) color turns.｜Where an instrument's name is uncertain, write its timbre, so that guessing does not pass for recognition.
Musical Form as Relations Between Materials｜Labeling similar and contrasting passages A and B helps you watch the structure, but you should also record what changes in each return. Formal analysis cannot stop at pasting on the name of a form.｜When the A section returns with denser orchestration and shortened rhythms, it works differently than at the opening.｜For a work of three to five minutes, draw a time chart marking three statements of the material and their specific differences.｜Section divisions have audible grounds, and you can explain why one boundary sits where it does.
Motif and Arranging Experiments｜Restricting yourself to small material helps you hear the choices. Change only one of rhythm, pitch direction, register, or timbre, then compare the results, instead of piling in every effect at once.｜Slowing the same motif and spreading its register may change its sense of weight; this is an experimental hypothesis, to be judged by actual listening.｜With humming, tapping, or software, make six variants of a ten-second motif and arrange them into a one-minute short piece.｜State your reasons for keeping or discarding variants; no expensive equipment or particular software is required.
Master Listening and the Independent Sound Project｜Advanced listening compares versions, interpretations, and recording conditions. Both creating and critiquing need a clear basis in the material; the harmonic language of a single tradition cannot stand for all music.｜Two performances of the same work differing in tempo and in the handling of phrase endings does not automatically mean one is right and the other wrong.｜Compare two reliable recordings of the same piece, then study one work from a different tradition; complete a five-minute sound project or a 2,500-word critique with time points.｜Have someone familiar with the relevant tradition respond, state the limits of your own hearing, and revise the structure or the argument.
''',
'09': '''
An Observation Vocabulary for Movement｜Recording movement by body part, direction, speed, sense of weight, and fluidity reduces vague emotion words. Watching and analyzing is not a prescription for how to move; practice begins with small, comfortable movements.｜The same raised arm, moved slowly and continuously or stopped suddenly, forms a different temporal texture, and meaning still depends on context.｜While seated, compose four hand movements, write five descriptive entries for each, then ask someone else to reproduce them from your notes.｜The notes help reproduction without requiring others to match the range of your own body.
Weight and Spatial Pathways｜Dance space includes extension near the body, traveling paths, and relative positions. Draw the paths and facings first, then watch how the movements connect; greatest extension is not the standard of quality.｜A circle and a zigzag, even using the same gesture, change the visual direction; a camera's viewpoint can hide the difference.｜With your hand or a paper figure, trace three paths—circle, straight line, zigzag—and record from two viewpoints.｜Distinguish the actual path from the camera's projection, and keep the body comfortable.
Developing a Movement Motif｜A movement motif can develop through repetition, reversal, enlargement, reduction, and change of rhythm. Changes should relate to the question, not use up every technique at once.｜Reaching, then a shortened reach, then stopping halfway can form a progressively constrained relationship.｜Make four variants of an eight-beat hand motif, choose two to combine, and explain the choice.｜Viewers can recognize the shared material and see the change at the same time.
Group Relations and Viewing Position｜Unison, alternation, counterpoint, and gathering and scattering organize group relations. Camera position and viewing direction also affect which structures are visible; dance video cannot fully stand in for the live event.｜Seen from the side, two overlapping dancers may read as one silhouette; from the front, the echo between them becomes visible.｜With two paper figures, design three sections of group choreography, marking each section's timing, positions, and audience direction.｜Every choice of direction has a basis, and movement the camera cannot see is not treated as nonexistent.
Working Methods of Choreography｜Choreography can iterate from a theme, movement material, constraints, and feedback from showings. Keep a passage small enough that viewers can describe the relationships they actually saw, then revise.｜If the theme was distance but viewers remember only speed, the spatial relations may need adjusting, or the new focus may need to be accepted explicitly.｜Complete one minute of low-intensity choreography for the hands or tabletop figures, hold two rounds of showings, and record the viewers' words and your revisions.｜Feedback does not stop at collecting likes and dislikes; respond to at least one gap between intention and experience.
Critical Study of Mature Choreography｜Studying a dance work means recording its version, performers, occasion, manner of viewing, and cultural source. Technical training requires an in-person teacher; texts and recordings mainly support observation, dancemaking, and reflection.｜The same passage danced by different dancers may show different breath and effort; a whole work cannot be judged from an edited fragment.｜Analyze three passages from each of two complete dance works; complete a five-minute choreographic plan or a timecoded study, with a movement score and two rounds of feedback.｜Check terms and background with a practitioner familiar with the tradition; this study does not replace physical training in technique.
''',
'10': '''
Action Verbs and Subtext｜Replace an abstract personality with the action of this moment: to persuade, to deflect, to probe, to block. Subtext is not a matter of the actor privately imagining ever more; it must form recognizable choices within the interaction between partners.｜"Please sit" can be soothing or can be controlling the conversation; it needs support from pauses, distance, and the other person's response.｜Play one line as three different actions, recording 30 seconds of each, and ask someone to describe the relationship they saw.｜The feedback speaks of concrete actions, and reading minds is not treated as the only right answer.
The Spatial Logic of Blocking｜Blocking, facing, distance, and levels organize relations and decide what the audience can see. Every move should have a function in the scene; not every action needs a realistic justification.｜One actor constantly blocking the doorway can form a relation of obstruction; if the door is invisible from another audience position, the meaning changes.｜For a scene in which two people contend for one chair, draw three versions of the blocking plan and mark the audience directions.｜The drawings show changes of sightline and relation, not just the track of moving bodies.
Xiqu Conventions and Specific Traditions｜Conventions (conventionalized forms) are learnable resources for expression, not one code shared by every opera genre. Research should be delimited by opera genre, role category, and the specific performance version, with practitioners' accounts given priority.｜One gesture need not carry the same meaning in different opera genres and situations; a single production still cannot explain the whole tradition.｜Choose a complete officially released excerpt, verify the opera genre and role category, and record three questions on movement and two on voice and sound.｜Unknown terms are kept explicitly open and filled in only after verification; rules for role categories are not invented.
How Design Joins the Theatrical Action｜Lighting, sound, costume, and scenery can supply information, shift focus, or establish conventions. A design should correspond to concrete scenes, not just carry adjectives of atmosphere.｜A sudden blackout may mark a shift in time or the consequence of a character's action; the script and the structure of the performance must decide.｜For a two-minute scene, write lighting and sound cue sheets listing trigger points, effects, purposes, and alternatives.｜The cues can be executed from the sheet, and each item has a theatrical function.
Rehearsal as the Test of Choices｜Rehearsal turns hypotheses into action; director and actors need a shared, workable language. Record each round of attempts and the audience's response, and avoid turning personal preference into an order that cannot be discussed.｜"A little sadder" is hard to execute; "pause before the last word and look toward the door" can be tested.｜With a short scene you wrote yourself, run three rounds of tabletop or low-cost rehearsal, changing one thing per round, and keep rehearsal notes and recordings.｜Discard at least one treatment you originally liked but that did not hold up, and explain the reason.
Master Performances and Full Productions｜Analyzing a mature performance connects script, acting, blocking, and design, and names the specific production. An independent production must also weigh rehearsal, technical needs, personnel, and the audience's experience.｜Two productions of the same script may pose different questions; one director's treatment cannot be equated with the text's only meaning.｜Compare excerpts from two productions of the same play; complete a ten-minute short-play plan or an actual small production, with script, blocking plan, cue sheets, and a retrospective.｜Have the audience recount the changes in relationship and have an experienced viewer review the work; revisions must answer specific evidence from the scenes.
''',
'11': '''
Spatial Continuity Across Shots｜Eyeline, direction of movement, and positional cues help the audience rebuild the space. Continuity rules are usable tools; departing from them can also be purposeful, but you need to know what the departure changes.｜After a character exits toward the right of the frame, entering from the left in the next shot is often read as continued travel; the specific scene still decides.｜With paper storyboards, plan a six-shot sequence of entering a room to fetch something, and ask someone to describe the space from the drawings alone.｜The story can be followed; if disorientation is created, state what purpose that disorientation serves.
Sound Leading the Image and Sound-Image Relations｜Sound can arrive before the picture changes or carry across a cut, linking scenes and guiding expectation. Whether the sound source is visible and whether it belongs to the story world (diegesis) are two separate axes of classification.｜Hearing the water of the next scene before cutting to the river builds the association early; a sound is not a score merely because its source is momentarily invisible.｜For the same static short film, design three sound timelines, marking each sound's source, entrance, exit, and narrative function.｜Sound classifications stay consistent throughout, and the design does not rest on mood labels alone.
Duration and Omission in Editing｜Editing chooses which moments are kept, left out, or repeated. Rhythm is not the same as cutting fast; a long take can carry variation and tension within itself.｜Opening a door can be shown in full, cut in the middle of the action, or elided straight to the room, producing different densities of information.｜Cut the same self-shot material into 30-second and 60-second versions, noting the work each omission does.｜The two versions are not a mechanical change of speed; you can explain how the timing of the audience's knowledge has changed.
Timing and Spacing in Animation｜Key poses, the spacing of movement, and pauses organize motion together. More frames are not automatically more natural; in teaching, one small action can first be studied with flipbooks or simple stop motion.｜Spacing an object's travel from small to large and back to small can show acceleration and deceleration; a real sense of weight also depends on deformation and sound.｜With 12 to 24 sheets of paper or photographs, animate one object's travel in two versions: even speed and varied spacing.｜Each version keeps its frame sequence, and you can point to the changes of position rather than only say it looks smoother.
From Question to Short Film｜A short film first delimits one filmable question, a few locations, and a clear audiovisual strategy. For documentary, ethics precede style: consent to film, the boundary between staging and observation, the stance of the commentary, and the subject's right of representation must all be thought through before the camera rolls. Script, shooting, and editing should orbit the same core; the scale of production is not the measure of depth.｜A short film about waiting can be conveyed through a repeated camera setup and step-by-step change, without depending on a character's explanation.｜Complete a two-minute short film: one core question, storyboard, shooting log, rough cut, and revised cut, with a record of the sound sources.｜A preview viewer can point to one piece of audiovisual evidence; their reading is not required to match yours.
Master Sequences and Independent Director Study｜Masterwork analysis records information, duration, sound, and transitions shot by shot, then explains how these choices work together. Collecting only beautiful frame grabs misses the film's organization in time.｜A seemingly calm shot may grow tense through the editing around it; a single frame cannot stand for the sequence.｜Analyze one complete sequence from each of two directors; complete a five- to ten-minute personal short film or a 2,500-word study with a shot list.｜Gather feedback separately from audiences and from people with production experience; after revising, submit the finished film, the process, and the unresolved questions.
''',
'12': '''
Drawing States and Transitions｜An interactive system can be written as current state, input, transition condition, and feedback. Drawing the flow before implementing it helps reveal dead responses, loops, or states with no way out.｜A button should have distinct states before and after selection; changing color alone may not be enough for everyone to understand.｜Design a three-state interaction on paper, invite someone to operate it, and record the feedback they expected but did not get.｜The states are distinguishable, and the error and exit paths can be explained.
Randomness and Reproducible Generation｜A generative system consists of rules, parameters, and selection. Keeping the inputs and the random seed helps reproduce a result; a system without randomness can also be generative art.｜The same rule with different parameters yields a family of related results; keeping only the best one hides the behavior of the system.｜Generate 20 line compositions with dice or a program, record the rules, keep every result, and explain your criteria for selection.｜Someone else can redo the work from the rules, and the labor of selection is not hidden.
Game Mechanics and Expression｜Mechanics organize choices through resources, constraints, and feedback. A theme should enter the consequences of action, not only the opening text; check as well whether a single obviously best solution appears.｜A game that claims to be about sharing but always rewards hoarding may convey, through its rules, values different from its copy.｜Build a three-round paper game, playtest five sessions, record the distribution of choices and the ignored options, and change one rule.｜Explain how the change affects the trade-offs, beyond adjusting the interface decoration.
Participant Research and Accessibility｜Observing task completion, misunderstanding, and exit is more useful than only asking whether people felt immersed. Captions, keyboard operation, contrast, and controllable sound can widen participation, but they should be tested in practice.｜Key information that appears only on mouse hover may be unreachable for touch-screen or keyboard users.｜Run three task tests on a paper or digital prototype, list input methods and alternative feedback, and revise the most severe obstacle.｜Record what was actually observed alongside what still needs verification; do not claim full accessibility from a checklist.
Process Archives for AI and Data｜Only by recording tool versions, source material, inputs, generation batches, selection, and manual modification can authorial choices be discussed. Image similarity is not sufficient evidence of where data came from.｜Several images from one prompt—the final combination and modification are creative decisions, but they do not automatically settle the rights in the source material.｜Complete a small generative project and build a source table, a parameter table, the full set of candidates, the reasons for selection, and before-and-after comparisons of your edits.｜Disclose the sources you could not confirm and the limits on reproduction; do not fabricate the process.
Independent System Works and Preservation｜A mature digital work must also state its runtime environment, failures, resets, and future re-creation. When studying classics, distinguish the live experience, the video document, and the runnable version.｜A change in a network interface can leave a work broken; video can preserve the appearance but not necessarily the relations of interaction.｜Study three institutionally documented digital or game works; complete a runnable or demonstrable prototype, an operations manual, tests, an archive, and an artist's statement.｜Test with first-time users and deliver after revision; state which experiences are available only through the original system.
''',
'13': '''
Writing Tradition Into Everyday Life｜Recording the maker, the community, the occasion, and the time keeps a tradition from being described as an unchanging specimen. A community may hold several views within it; a single interview cannot represent everyone.｜The same lantern may carry different participants and meanings in a family setting, a festival, and a commercial exhibition.｜Make a research card for one festive object in your home and hold one voluntary interview, distinguishing firsthand experience, secondhand account, and your own interpretation.｜Dissenting views are kept, and personal information is not published without consent.
The Chain from Material to Performance｜Folk forms often link making, telling, sound, manipulation, and occasion of use. Classification eases retrieval, but research cannot keep only the collectible object.｜A photograph of a shadow puppet's form does not include its manipulation and sung narration; full understanding still requires watching the performance and hearing practitioners.｜Draw the process chain of one familiar form, marking materials, makers, users, sound, and occasion, and locate one gap in the documentation.｜The chain includes actual activity; the object's appearance is not taken for the whole of the experience.
Adaptation Must State Its Relationships｜When a traditional form is used in a new project, state where the borrowing comes from, what was changed, and who has a chance to speak. Respect cannot rest on one line of tribute; it must show in the process and the presentation.｜Borrowing a pattern for a poster may change its meaning if the original context of use is stripped away; this needs to be recorded, not pretended away.｜Choose one publicly usable form for a paper adaptation, with sources, a table of changes, and questions you would put to practitioners.｜Do not claim to represent the community, and do not invent the approval of practitioners.
Rules and Records of a Performance Work｜In performance art the medium may be the body, duration, repetition, or relation. Clear rules let the work be discussed; the record is only one form in which the work survives and cannot be equated with the original event.｜Repeatedly tidying the same sheet of paper for one minute lets the audience watch the action vary; its value still depends on whether the question and the experience hold.｜Do a one-minute desktop action that affects no one else; write the rules, a process log, and the gains and losses of two ways of recording.｜You can distinguish the three kinds of material: event, artist's statement, and record.
Interfaces of Intermedia Collaboration｜Collaboration requires stating what function each medium carries, how the handoffs happen, and how decisions are made. Adding sound and image is not necessarily richer than a single medium, and they may screen one another.｜If the words already carry all the information, the voice-over may merely repeat them; a deliberate divergence is also possible, but the reason must be stated.｜Make one theme in three versions—paper, sound, and movement—choose two combinations, and test which part of the information cannot be replaced.｜Cut one ineffective medium, or give a concrete reason for keeping the repetition.
Long-Term Social and Intermedia Projects｜A mature project attends to the participants' experience, the author's power, the relationships after the project ends, and the archive. Artistic impact cannot rest on the artist's declaration; it requires bounded evidence and time frames.｜Participants enjoying one workshop does not prove long-term social change; the experience of that session can be described honestly.｜Study three intermedia practices with process documentation; complete a small voluntary project or a full proposal, with roles, rules, records, and a retrospective.｜Give participants a chance to respond to how they are represented, revise according to the feedback, and note the long-term effects you cannot assess.
''',
}

LEVELS = [('Introductory', '01–03', 'Can describe artworks with evidence and separate observation from inference.'), ('Foundational', '04–05', 'Can run controlled comparisons and keep exercise records that can be redone.'), ('Intermediate', '06–07', 'Can apply the methods of a category to analyze, test, and revise.'), ('Advanced', '08', 'Can complete a focused project with constraints, process records, and feedback.'), ('Independent project and masterwork study', '09', 'Can study mature works, complete an independent project, and respond to outside criticism.')]
